     Text moves between windows in two independent ways.

     The quick way is the _s_e_l_e_c_t_i_o_n: sweep over text with the
     lleefftt button held down - it highlights - and release; the
     swept text becomes the PRIMARY selection.  A mmiiddddllee click
     then pastes it into the window _u_n_d_e_r _t_h_e _p_o_i_n_t_e_r at the
     click position, deliberately _w_i_t_h_o_u_t raising that window, so
     text can be carried into a partly covered window without
     disturbing the stacking.  Only one window at a time shows a
     selection highlight; making a new selection clears the old
     one, but the stored text remains until replaced, so it can
     be pasted repeatedly.  This works in both directions between
     any windows that support selection - sweep in a terminal and
     middle-click into the editor, or sweep in the editor (or the
     manual browser) and middle-click into a terminal.

     The durable way is the _c_l_i_p_b_o_a_r_d, driven by the CCuutt, CCooppyy
     and PPaassttee entries an application puts in its window menu (or
     their function keys, where it has them).  It is a separate
     store: it survives further selections and is explicit -
     nothing reaches the clipboard until a Cut or Copy.  In a
     terminal window, CCooppyy puts the current selection on the


